    "body": "Walk through a Korean city and you may see signs covered with shapes that look like squares, circles, and short lines. Those shapes are letters in Hangul, the alphabet used to write Korean. The letters may be unfamiliar to someone who reads only English, but they follow an organized system. Many Hangul letters are grouped into blocks that represent syllables, making words look like neat clusters of symbols rather than a line of separate letters.\n\n## A new writing system\n\nHangul was created in the fifteenth century during the reign of King Sejong of Korea's Joseon dynasty. At the time, written communication often used Chinese characters, which could be difficult for many ordinary people to learn. The new alphabet was designed to represent Korean speech more directly. Its creation is remembered as an important moment in the history of Korean language and education because it offered a different approach to making written words accessible.\n\n## Shapes connected to sounds\n\nSome original Hangul consonant letters were designed with reference to positions of the tongue or mouth used when making sounds. Vowel letters were organized according to a separate set of principles. Modern Hangul contains letters for consonants and vowels, which are combined into syllable blocks. For example, a syllable beginning with one consonant and ending with a vowel may be written as one square-like cluster. This organization is one reason Hangul looks visually different from the Roman alphabet used in English.\n\n## Writing is not the same as speaking\n\nLearning to recognize letters is a first step, but reading a language also requires vocabulary and understanding. Someone might learn to sound out Hangul letters without knowing what a Korean sentence means. That is similar to reading a word in another language using familiar letters but not knowing its meaning. Language learning involves listening, speaking, reading, and writing together. Different students may progress at different speeds.\n\n## Language and culture\n\nKorean appears in songs, films, games, books, and everyday conversations. International interest in Korean media has encouraged many people to study the language. Yet the language has a history much older than modern television or music. Its writing system connects today's readers to generations of people who used it for letters, literature, news, and education. Studying Hangul can open a way to learn about Korean history as well as current culture.\n\n## Try noticing patterns\n\nFind a printed example of Hangul from a reliable language-learning source. Look for repeated symbols in different syllable blocks. You may recognize the same consonant or vowel appearing in several words. Do not guess the meaning based only on a picture; use a dictionary or teacher's help. The activity shows how an unfamiliar system begins to make sense when a learner notices repeated patterns and tests what they might mean.",
