    "body": "Take a square sheet of paper and fold one corner neatly toward another. The paper becomes smaller in one direction and gains a sharp line called a crease. With more folds, the same sheet might become a boat, an animal, or an intricate geometric figure. Origami is the art of paper folding, strongly associated with Japanese traditions and practiced by people worldwide. The result can look like magic, but each transformation follows a sequence of understandable steps.\n\n## Paper and a long tradition\n\nPaper folding has appeared in different cultures, and Japanese origami developed over many generations. Some folded forms became connected to ceremonies or gifts, while others became popular recreational activities. The paper crane is one of the most recognizable designs. For many people, making a crane represents patience and care. A tradition can carry different meanings for different families and communities, so learning its history is more useful than assuming every folded shape has one fixed story.\n\n## Geometry at your fingertips\n\nEach fold changes the relationships between points, lines, and angles. When two corners meet, the crease can divide a sheet into matching shapes. When a square is folded diagonally, the result includes triangles. A student might explore symmetry by folding paper and checking whether two halves line up. Even a simple paper airplane uses geometry because the shape of its wings affects how air moves around it. Paper folding turns abstract lines on a diagram into physical objects that can be touched.\n\n## Following directions closely\n\nAn origami instruction might say to fold a corner to the center, turn the paper over, or make a valley fold. A valley fold bends the paper toward you so the crease looks like a valley. A mountain fold bends it the opposite way. Small differences matter because later steps depend on earlier ones. If one fold is misaligned, the finished design may not look quite right. Learning to correct a mistake without tearing the paper is part of the process.\n\n## Engineers study folds too\n\nFolding ideas are useful beyond crafts. Engineers investigate ways to pack large objects into small spaces and unfold them when needed. Foldable solar panels and space structures use related principles, although their materials and mechanisms can be much more complex than paper. Mathematicians also study which crease patterns can fold flat. Origami gives students an approachable way to explore big questions about shape and movement.\n\n## Make a geometry journal\n\nChoose a simple origami pattern from a trusted educational source and write down the steps you follow. After each fold, sketch the new shape and name any triangles, squares, or lines of symmetry you notice. If a step goes wrong, explain what changed instead of simply starting over in frustration. That journal can show how careful observation, clear instructions, and repeated practice turn one flat sheet into a three-dimensional creation.",
